1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,960
At 3,000 rpm, a car engine's crankshaft turns 50 times a second.

2
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:13,000
Yet each cylinder burns fuel only 25 times a second: once every two turns.

3
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:18,840
Its piston makes four trips for every burn, and only one of them pushes.

4
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:24,260
This is the four-stroke cycle, first patented by a man who never built one

5
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:30,460
and made to work by a former grocery salesman, who later lost his patent.

6
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:38,080
Let's open one up. This is a simplified diagram of a single cylinder.

7
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:41,630
Inside the cylinder, a piston slides up and down.

8
00:00:41,710 --> 00:00:45,970
A connecting rod links the piston to the crankshaft, which turns.

9
00:00:46,050 --> 00:00:51,390
At the top, two valves: one lets air and fuel in, the other lets burnt gas out.

10
00:00:51,470 --> 00:00:55,790
Cams on a camshaft push them open, and springs close them again.

11
00:00:55,870 --> 00:00:58,610
Between them sits the spark plug.

12
00:00:58,690 --> 00:01:04,030
The piston's highest point is top dead centre; its lowest, bottom dead centre.

13
00:01:04,110 --> 00:01:07,580
One trip between them, up or down, is a stroke.

14
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:12,550
As the crank turns, the piston follows, but not in a perfectly even rhythm.

15
00:01:12,630 --> 00:01:17,320
After a quarter turn, the piston has already covered more than half its stroke.

16
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:23,950
The tilting rod makes it hurry through the top half of the stroke, and dawdle through the bottom.

17
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:30,890
Now let's run one cycle, slowly, from the top.

18
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:37,340
Stroke 1: intake. The intake valve opens, and the falling piston draws in air and fuel.

19
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:45,080
Stroke 2: compression. Both valves close, and the rising piston squeezes the mixture into about a tenth of the space.

20
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:48,850
By now the crankshaft has turned once, all the way round.

21
00:01:48,930 --> 00:01:53,370
Near the top, the spark plug fires, and the mixture starts to burn.

22
00:01:53,450 --> 00:01:58,340
Stroke 3: power. The hot, high-pressure gas drives the piston down.

23
00:01:58,420 --> 00:02:01,560
This is the only stroke that pushes on the crank.

24
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:09,130
Stroke 4: exhaust. The exhaust valve opens, and the rising piston pushes the burnt gas out.

25
00:02:09,210 --> 00:02:13,920
Then it all starts again. Four strokes, two turns of the crank, one push.

26
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,840
The camshaft turns at half the crankshaft's speed, so each valve opens once per cycle.

27
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:24,710
What carries the piston through the three strokes that don't push? A heavy flywheel.

28
00:02:24,790 --> 00:02:31,390
It soaks up energy from each power stroke, and gives some back to squeeze the next charge.

29
00:02:34,990 --> 00:02:38,500
The idea is older than the engine that made it famous.

30
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:46,320
In 1860, Étienne Lenoir in Paris built a gas engine that burned its fuel without compressing it first.

31
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:50,540
It turned only about 4% of the fuel's energy into work.

32
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:57,330
A travelling salesman in Germany, Nicolaus Otto, learned of it, and started building engines himself.

33
00:02:57,410 --> 00:03:05,070
In 1862 he tried one that compressed its charge first. It ran for a few minutes, then destroyed itself.

34
00:03:05,150 --> 00:03:12,390
That same year, in France, Alphonse Beau de Rochas patented the four-stroke cycle. But he never built an engine.

35
00:03:12,470 --> 00:03:19,010
In 1864, Otto and Eugen Langen founded the first company devoted to making engines.

36
00:03:19,090 --> 00:03:27,710
Their atmospheric engine, at about 12% efficiency, won a gold medal at the Paris World's Fair of 1867.

37
00:03:27,790 --> 00:03:34,610
Then, in 1876, Otto's company built an engine that compressed its charge in four strokes.

38
00:03:34,690 --> 00:03:41,900
It was an immediate success: the first commercially successful engine to compress its charge in the cylinder.

39
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:49,320
Two of Otto's former engineers, Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach, made it small and fast enough for vehicles.

40
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:56,640
But in 1886 the patent was cancelled in Germany, because Beau de Rochas had patented the cycle first.

41
00:03:56,720 --> 00:04:02,090
By 1889, more than 50 companies were building Otto-type engines.

42
00:04:02,170 --> 00:04:06,970
And the cycle still carries his name: the Otto cycle.

43
00:04:10,570 --> 00:04:16,680
Why does squeezing first help so much? Engineers answer with a graph of pressure against volume.

44
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:21,950
This is the idealised version, the Otto cycle, traced as our engine turns.

45
00:04:22,030 --> 00:04:26,000
Intake: the volume grows, at about the pressure outside.

46
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:30,920
Compression: the volume shrinks tenfold, and the pressure climbs steeply.

47
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,190
Combustion is treated as instant, at the top: the pressure leaps while the volume stays the same.

48
00:04:37,270 --> 00:04:41,640
Power: the hot gas expands and pushes, and its pressure falls.

49
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:46,090
At the bottom, the exhaust valve opens and the pressure drops back.

50
00:04:46,170 --> 00:04:51,210
Exhaust: the piston sweeps the gas out, and the loop closes.

51
00:04:51,290 --> 00:04:58,910
The area inside the loop is the net work: what the power stroke gave, minus what the compression cost.

52
00:05:02,710 --> 00:05:08,150
The compression ratio is the cylinder's largest volume divided by its smallest.

53
00:05:08,230 --> 00:05:12,750
In the ideal cycle, the efficiency depends on that ratio alone.

54
00:05:12,830 --> 00:05:19,790
Efficiency is 1 minus r to the power 1 minus γ, where γ, gamma, is 1.4 for air.

55
00:05:19,870 --> 00:05:25,410
Squeeze four to one, and the ideal engine turns 43% of its heat into work.

56
00:05:25,490 --> 00:05:31,550
At ten to one, 60%. At sixteen to one, 67%.

57
00:05:31,630 --> 00:05:39,500
Real engines lose heat and friction along the way: a petrol engine turns about 30% into useful work.

58
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:44,600
But the lesson holds: squeeze harder, and you get more work from the same fuel.

59
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:49,640
So why do most petrol car engines stop at around 8 to 12 to one?

60
00:05:49,720 --> 00:05:54,310
Squeezing a gas heats it, and a hot enough mixture can ignite by itself.

61
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:58,760
Normally, the spark starts a flame front that sweeps across the chamber.

62
00:05:58,840 --> 00:06:04,730
But if the unburnt mixture ahead of the flame gets too hot, pockets of it explode all at once.

63
00:06:04,810 --> 00:06:10,000
That's knock: a metallic pinging, and pressure spikes that can damage the engine.

64
00:06:10,080 --> 00:06:17,620
A fuel's octane rating measures how well it resists igniting by itself. Higher octane allows more squeeze.

65
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:24,800
And modern engines listen for knock with sensors, and fire the spark later when they hear it.

66
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:34,720
So far, everything happened exactly at the top or bottom of a stroke. Real engines don't wait.

67
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:40,390
The spark fires early: some 10 to 40 degrees of crank rotation before the top.

68
00:06:40,470 --> 00:06:45,490
The flame takes time to burn, and the peak pressure should come just after the top.

69
00:06:45,570 --> 00:06:51,930
The flame doesn't speed up with the engine, so at higher revs the spark needs a longer head start.

70
00:06:52,010 --> 00:06:57,380
The valves are early, too. They open before the end of a stroke, and close after it.

71
00:06:57,460 --> 00:07:03,070
At the end of the exhaust stroke, both valves are briefly open at once: valve overlap.

72
00:07:03,150 --> 00:07:08,590
The outgoing exhaust is moving fast, and it helps draw the fresh charge in behind it.

73
00:07:08,670 --> 00:07:14,190
Designers aim to close the exhaust valve just as the fresh charge reaches it.

74
00:07:14,270 --> 00:07:23,080
Racing engines use lots of overlap. Road engines, which must idle smoothly below 1,000 rpm, use less.

75
00:07:23,160 --> 00:07:30,350
Many now shift their camshafts as they run, to get both: power at high revs, and a smooth idle.

76
00:07:33,950 --> 00:07:39,470
One cylinder gives one push every two turns. That's a lumpy way to drive a car.

77
00:07:39,550 --> 00:07:44,840
So most car engines have several cylinders on one crankshaft, firing in turn.

78
00:07:44,920 --> 00:07:52,030
Spaced evenly, the power strokes come every 720 degrees divided by the number of cylinders.

79
00:07:52,110 --> 00:07:57,680
With four cylinders, that's every 180 degrees: a push every half turn.

80
00:07:57,760 --> 00:08:03,100
A straight four usually fires its cylinders in the order 1, 3, 4, 2.

81
00:08:03,180 --> 00:08:08,370
Here is the twisting force that the gas puts on the crank, in an idealised model.

82
00:08:08,450 --> 00:08:13,860
One cylinder: one big shove, then a long wait, with a drag while it compresses.

83
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:17,030
Four cylinders: one shove after another.

84
00:08:17,110 --> 00:08:22,850
Six cylinders: the power strokes overlap, and the push is smoother still.

85
00:08:22,930 --> 00:08:28,070
Even spacing means less vibration and a smoother sound.

86
00:08:31,670 --> 00:08:35,210
There is another way to light the fire: no spark at all.

87
00:08:35,290 --> 00:08:41,880
A diesel draws in only air, and squeezes it much harder, typically 15 to 23 to one.

88
00:08:41,960 --> 00:08:45,530
Squeezing air that hard heats it by hundreds of degrees.

89
00:08:45,610 --> 00:08:50,550
Near the top, fuel is sprayed in, and it ignites in the hot air by itself.

90
00:08:50,630 --> 00:08:57,400
With no fuel in the cylinder during the squeeze, nothing can ignite early, so a diesel can squeeze harder.

91
00:08:57,480 --> 00:09:03,920
That's one reason diesels are efficient. Big, slow ship diesels reach up to 55%.

92
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,790
And instead of throttling its air, a diesel sets its power by how much fuel it injects.

93
00:09:09,870 --> 00:09:20,610
Rudolf Diesel's first prototype fired in 1893. His third, tested in 1897, turned about 26% of its fuel into work.

94
00:09:20,690 --> 00:09:26,810
And it runs the same four strokes: intake, compression, power, exhaust.

95
00:09:30,410 --> 00:09:32,450
So here is the whole idea.

96
00:09:32,530 --> 00:09:37,220
Draw in, squeeze, burn, push out: four strokes, two turns of the crank.

97
00:09:37,300 --> 00:09:41,490
The squeeze makes it efficient, and knock sets the limit on the squeeze.

98
00:09:41,570 --> 00:09:48,910
Patented in 1862 by a man who never built one, made to work by Otto in 1876

99
00:09:48,990 --> 00:09:52,580
it is still the cycle inside most car engines.

100
00:09:52,660 --> 00:10:00,580
At 3,000 rpm, that's two turns and one push, 25 times every second.
